Restart the echo canceller for a microphone turned on again in a call, keeping what it knows of the room

Patch 0009 made AEC3 anew each time the audio device started recording again: at a call's
start, and when a microphone turned off (the voice channel only receiving) is turned on
again. A new AEC3 knows nothing of the room. With a headset there is no echo, and AEC3 only
lets the microphone through untouched while the other side talks once its transparent mode
decides that there is none: the legacy classifier waits for 6 s of the other side's sound
without a converged filter (TransparentMode, strong_not_saturated_render_blocks_ over
6 * kNumBlocksPerSecond). Until then its suppressor treats the microphone as echo whenever
the other side's sound plays. Measured in the call lab with a headset and the other side
holding a tone: after each unmute the first words went out about 17 dB weaker and the
room's noise about 10 dB lower for 3 to 6 s, which then came back at once, as a step.

Only where nothing can have changed the room is it kept. AudioState tells a microphone
turned on again in the call recording stopped in (the same streams received and played
all along since: stopped_in_call_, which any stream heard coming or going, playout or
recording turned off, ends) from a call's start, and only there asks the audio
processing to restart its echo controller for recording
(AudioProcessing::RestartEchoControllerForRecording, EchoControl::RestartForRecording); a
call's start makes it anew as 0009 did (RestartEchoController, unchanged): between calls
the room and the devices may have changed. Patch 0011 makes it anew after a change of audio
devices too.

Restarted, AEC3 drops the played sound queued while nothing was recorded, and at its next
recorded block resets its render buffer and delay estimator as a new AEC3 does at its first,
and its echo remover as at a delay change (kBufferFlush: filters, ERLE, initial state,
suppressor's initial state), which is all the alignment the stale state of 0009 was about;
but whether there is an echo path at all (transparent mode) is the room's, not the
alignment's, and is kept (EchoPathVariability::recording_restarted,
AecState::HandleEchoPathChange). As a new AEC3, the restarted one processes nothing before
fresh played sound arrives (render_properly_started_). Another echo controller still is
made anew.

AudioProcessingImpl restarts it with both its locks held (EchoCanceller3 checks its race
checkers). The request still comes after the audio device's StartRecording returns, so
that a recording that fails to start never restarts it (wrapper patches 0018 and 0021);
the capture thread may process a frame or two before it, which the restart then realigns.

Measured in the call lab with wrapper patch 0019 (mic.sh unmute), as first written, when
every start restarted AEC3: with a headset and the other side holding a tone, the first
word after an unmute went out at 0 dB against the later ones (-8 dB on average before, -17
at worst) and the room's noise in the first 0.5 s at 0 dB (-12 before). Without
render_properly_started_ a call's start sent the other side's voice back for 5.5 s at the
median (10 runs); forcing an echo path change at the first delay found after a restart,
keeping wrapper patch 0019's headroom after a reset and judging the transparent mode's sane
filter from that headroom were each tried and made a call's start worse. Making the render
buffer anew at the restart (it keeps the played blocks of the moment recording stopped,
which Reset only skips) was tried too: no unmute measured better, and one in six headset
checks failed.

Measured as it is, in one session of the call lab (mic.sh unmute, the other side talking
in speech-like noise that repeats only every 30 s, tone.py speech; with speakers unless said; 8 or 6
runs, medians; "heard": how long their voice came back above -50 dBFS; p of a rank test
against 0009 as published, all in the same session):
- a call's start: -70 dBFS at the loudest, heard 0 s (0009: -68 dBFS, 0 s; p 0.96 and
  0.47);
- a microphone on again in the call (16 unmutes): heard 0.4 s (0009: 0.3 s, p 0.28); with
  a headset (unmute:headset, 6 runs) the first word went out at 0 dB against the later
  ones, as in every run since this patch was first written;
- a second call in the same process: heard 0.4 s (0009: 0.3 s, p 0.44);
- a headset taken off for speakers while the microphone was off, with no change of devices
  anyone could tell (the cost of keeping the room): -15 dBFS, heard 1.1 s (0.3 to 2.2)
  against -32 dBFS and 0.2 s with 0009 (p 0.01); with the device's change marked (patch
  0011), as 0009;
- the other side talking over one's words: cut as much as with 0009, and later in a call
  less (16 % of each word against 37 %).
In the lab's logs a new AEC3 and a restarted one find the same delay (7 blocks with wrapper
patch 0019's headroom) within 0.2 to 1.5 s and converge as fast; the restart is kept for
an unmute because it keeps the transparent mode, not because it aligns faster.

